  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains centered over the Four Corners\nthis morning, but shortwave energy moving over the topping of the\nfeature is weakening it and moving it a bit south. Meanwhile, a\nweak shortwave has moved onshore over northern California and will\ncontinue east across the Great Basin during the day. Ahead of it,\nUtah and southwest Wyoming will stay dry and very mild with highs\nmore typical of late August or early September. These maxes up to\n15F above seasonal normals will challenge records in some\nlocations, including Salt Lake City.\n\nThe shortwave is on track to make its way into northwest Utah\naround midnight, making its way across the remainder of Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming through Tuesday evening. This will act to curb\nthe warming trend just a bit, with maxes running a couple of\ndegrees cooler tomorrow compared to today. This will also bring in\njust enough moisture and instability for a few showers and\nthunderstorms, with the best chance Tuesday afternoon with the\nheating of the day over the higher terrain. The shortwave energy \nis expected to linger in the vicinity of the Four Corners for much\nof Wednesday, which will keep a small amount of instability in \nplace, so would not be surprised to see a shower or two develop \nWednesday afternoon. \n\nBehind the shortwave, dry and stable conditions are then expected\nthrough the first part of the weekend, with maxes staying around\n10F above seasonal normals. However, confidence is starting to\nincrease for a pattern change early next week. Guidance continues\nto show an unseasonably cold Pacific Northwest system moving\nonshore Sunday, with good agreement that this will push much\ncolder air in through Monday. There is much more spread with\nregard to the potential of precipitation, with some guidance\nallowing the system to tap into deeper moisture, while another\nchunk keeping things relatively dry. Will also need to keep an eye\non the potential for strong winds, particularly ahead of the\nfront, during the day Monday.",
